<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NKVD troika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Special troika, osobaya troyka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> troikas, or special troikas, were quasi-judicial commissions of three officials of the Soviet security police that sentenced people after simplified, speedy investigations and without a public trial, as an instrument of quick and secret execution or imprisonment. The first was set up in 1918 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>. From 1930 regional troikas of a police officer, a local party secretary and a procurator decided the fate of peasants branded kulaks, without appeal. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd-order-no-00447">NKVD Order No. 00447</a> of 1937 they condemned people to fill target numbers; when the operation was stopped in November 1938, up to 767,000 people had been condemned, of whom 387,000 were shot.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nkvd-troika.jpg" alt="NKVD troika" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NKVD_troika_execution_order_for_Samson_Dadiani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its camps held ordinary criminals and political prisoners, many sentenced without trial by <b>NKVD troikas</b>; petty crimes and jokes about the government could bring a term, and Article 58 punished “counterrevolutionary activities”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
